.TH CTRL-EXEC-AGENT 8 "2026-06-10" "ctrl-exec" "ctrl-exec Manual"
.SH NAME
ctrl-exec-agent \- mTLS remote script execution agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ctrl-exec-agent
.RI [ mode ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cea
.RI [ mode ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ctrl-exec-agent
runs on a managed host and executes only the scripts named in a per-host
allowlist, on request from a paired control host over HTTPS with mutual
certificate authentication. There is no shell: arguments are passed directly
to the named program. The agent listens on port 7443 by default and reloads
its configuration (except
.IR port ,
.IR cert ,
.I key
and
.IR ca ,
which need a restart), allowlist, revocation list, and trusted-dispatcher map on
.BR SIGHUP ,
and reloads its configuration and allowlist by itself at the next connection
after either file changes on disk.
A reload that cannot be applied leaves the previous configuration in force;
a scripts.conf entry naming an undefined profile skips only that script.
The agent may be paired with more than one dispatcher: each is recorded in the
trusted-dispatcher map and every request is attributed to the dispatcher that
made it.
.PP
.B cea
is a shortcut for
.BR ctrl-exec-agent .
.SH MODES
The first non-option argument selects a mode. Invoked with no mode,
.B ctrl-exec-agent
prints this usage summary and exits; to run the server pass
.B serve
explicitly.
.TP
.B serve
Start the agent server in the foreground.
.TP
.B request-pairing
Generate a key and CSR and submit a pairing request to a control host (see
.B \-\-dispatcher
below). After the operator approves, the signed certificate is stored. With
.BR \-\-dispatcher-fingerprint ,
the control host is verified before the CSR is sent. An agent already paired
keeps its key and certificate and presents them with their CA instead; the
control host pins that CA for this agent and signs nothing, so the control
hosts it is already paired with keep verifying it.
.TP
.B pairing-status
Show pairing and certificate status. Reads the root-only certificate files;
run as root (sudo).
.TP
.B self-check
Validate the local configuration, allowlist, and certificate files. No
network connection is made. Reads the root-only configuration; run as root
(sudo). Also notes whether the agent is running.
.TP
.B self-ping
Connect to the running agent on the loopback address over mTLS and send a
ping. A 403 serial-mismatch response is expected and reported as success: the
agent's own cert serial is not in its trusted-dispatcher map, so the request is
rejected - confirming the port is listening, the TLS stack works, and the agent
is enforcing its trusted-dispatcher policy. Requires the agent to be running and
paired; if nothing is listening on the configured port it reports that the agent
is not running and how to start it, and exits 1.
.TP
.BI remove-dispatcher " id"
Stop trusting one control host (root). Removes its trusted-map entries and
each of its CAs from the CA bundle unless another trusted control host is under
that CA, then sends SIGHUP to the running agent and logs
.BR ACTION=remove-dispatcher .
The map change, and a CA removed from the TLS listener, take effect at the
reload; no restart is needed. The last CA is never removed, so
removing the last control host leaves an agent that starts but refuses every
caller;
.B self-check
says so. The CA that issued the agent's own certificate is likewise kept (self-ping and
renewal need it); the command then warns that the removed control host can no longer
renew the certificate, and when it expires, so the agent should be re-paired. An unknown id changes nothing, exits 1 and names the known ids.
.TP
.B list-dispatchers
Print each trusted control host as one line,
.IR "id ca-fingerprint serial[,serial...] [issuer-of-agent-cert]" ,
sorted by id then CA (the fingerprint is
.B \-
for an old map line not yet tied to a CA). Root only.
.TP
.B doctor
List every problem the agent's files show, one line each:
.B PROBLEM
(something does not work as configured, or is unsafe) or
.B note
(works; worth knowing, or could not be checked), what was found, and the
command that fixes it. Checks agent.conf, every scripts.conf entry (skipped
and why, missing or not executable, the ownership rule under
.BR "script_ownership = warn" ,
unknown options,
.B dispatchers=
ids no paired control host has), profiles without the executor, the executor
socket, pairing and the certificate, and the trust state (map entries whose CA
is not in the bundle, CAs no entry uses, an old-format map, a certificate whose
issuing control host is no longer trusted, revocations under every CA). Each
finding is also logged
.RB ( ACTION=doctor-finding ).
Runs without root, reporting what it could not read as a note. Exit 0 when no
problem was found (notes may remain), 1 when one was, 2 on bad usage. Package
upgrades and
.B install.sh
run
.B "doctor \-\-brief"
after an upgrade and print the result; never
.BR \-\-fix .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
Path to
.BR agent.conf .
Default:
.IR /etc/ctrl-exec-agent/agent.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-allowlist " path"
Path to
.BR scripts.conf .
Default:
.IR /etc/ctrl-exec-agent/scripts.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-port " n"
Override the pairing port for
.BR request-pairing .
Default: 7444.
.TP
.BI \-\-dispatcher-fingerprint " SHA256:hex"
Verified pairing: the SHA-256 fingerprint of the control host's CA
certificate (from
.B "ctrl-exec-dispatcher ca-fingerprint"
or the
.B pairing-mode
banner; colons and either case accepted). The agent refuses, before sending
its CSR, a control host whose certificate does not chain to that CA. Use it on
any network you do not control.
.TP
.BI \-\-lookup-by " ip|hostname"
Suggest how the control host should resolve this agent for dispatch
(default: hostname). The control host's
.B "approve \-\-lookup-by"
overrides it.
.TP
.B \-\-rekey
With
.BR request-pairing :
generate a new key and have the control host sign it even when already
paired. The control hosts the agent is paired with then refuse the new
certificate until each is re-paired.
.TP
.BI \-\-pidfile " path"
For
.BR remove-dispatcher :
the pid file of the running agent to reload. Default: the init script's pid
file, else systemd's main pid.
.TP
.B \-\-background
Non-interactive pairing: print the request ID and wait for approval in the
background. For orchestrated installations.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " n"
Seconds to wait for approval in background mode (default 30, max 600).
.TP
.B \-\-fix
With
.BR doctor :
also make the fixes that cannot lose data or widen access, and list them as
.BR fixed :
an old-format trusted-dispatcher map is upgraded in place (as the agent does
when it starts), and a CA in the bundle that no trusted control host uses and
that did not issue the agent's certificate is removed (never the last one).
Nothing else is changed: permissions and ownership of script directories, for
example, are reported only. A second run changes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-json
With
.BR doctor :
print one JSON object on stdout:
.IR program ", " ok ", " problems ", " notes ", " fixed
and
.I findings
(each
.IR severity ", " check ", " what ", " fix ", " fixed ).
.TP
.B \-\-brief
With
.BR doctor :
list problems (and fixes) only, notes as a count; nothing to fix prints one
line.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ctrl-exec-agent/agent.conf
Agent configuration: listen port, certificate paths, optional
.BR allowed_ips ,
rate limiting, and a
.B [tags]
section.
.TP
.I /etc/ctrl-exec-agent/scripts.conf
Allowlist mapping script names to executable paths.
.TP
.I /opt/ctrl-exec-scripts
Default directory for agent scripts.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ctrl-exec-dispatcher (8),
.BR ctrl-exec-api (8).
.PP
Full reference: the REFERENCE document shipped with ctrl-exec and
.UR https://github.com/OpenDigitalCC/ctrl-exec
.UE .
